\begin{proof}
By the binomial theorem,
\[
\frac{(x+2)^p-2^p}{x}=\sum_{j=1}^{p}\binom pj2^{p-j}x^{j-1}
=x^{p-1}+\sum_{j=1}^{p-1}\binom pj2^{p-j}x^{j-1}.
\]
Since $p$ is prime, $p\mid\binom pj$ for every $1\le j\le p-1$. Thus every nonleading coefficient is divisible by $p$. The constant coefficient is $p2^{p-1}$, which is not divisible by $p^2$ because $p$ is odd. Hence Eisenstein's criterion at $p$ proves that the polynomial is irreducible in $\mathbb Z[x]$.
\end{proof}
